---
import Card from "astro-theme-university/components/Card.astro";
import CardGrid from "astro-theme-university/components/CardGrid.astro";
import ContentLayout from "astro-theme-university/layouts/ContentLayout.astro";
import { withBase } from "astro-theme-university/url";
import FridgeAudit from "../components/FridgeAudit.astro";
import heroImage from "../assets/images/hero-home.avif";
import sessionsHero from "../assets/images/sessions-hero.avif";
import lecturesHero from "../assets/images/lectures-hero.avif";
import assessmentsHero from "../assets/images/assessments-hero.avif";
import peopleHero from "../assets/images/people-hero.avif";
import policiesHero from "../assets/images/policies-hero.avif";
import { courseMeta } from "../course-config";
import { sessionLabels, siteConfig } from "../site-config";
---

<ContentLayout
  title={`${courseMeta.code}: ${courseMeta.title}`}
  description={courseMeta.description}
  heroImage={heroImage}
  heroImageAlt="The inside of a refrigerator, drawn flat in gold and teal on a warm near-black. Five shelves fill the frame from top to bottom, every one of them packed end to end with jars, bottles, cartons, egg boxes, wrapped parcels and sprigs of greens, continuing past both edges."
  {...siteConfig}
>
  <ul class="course-tags" aria-label="Course tags">
    {courseMeta.tags.map((tag) => <li>{tag}</li>)}
  </ul>

  <p>
    There is a machine in your kitchen that has run without interruption since
    the day you moved in. You did not choose its shape. You have never
    questioned its necessity. You would struggle to describe how a household
    managed before it existed — and that struggle is not a gap in your
    education. It is the product.
  </p>

  <FridgeAudit />

  <h2>The argument</h2>
  <p>
    This course takes one position and holds it for twelve weeks. The
    refrigerator is not a neutral appliance. It is the control centre of
    micropolitics in the modern household, and an <strong
      >illusory fortress</strong
    > against temporal decay. By manufacturing expiration anxiety and hoarding illusions,
    it colludes with and sustains late capitalism's logic of food waste and overconsumption.
  </p>
  <p>
    The fortress works, which is the awkward part. Food really is colder and
    really does last longer. It simply does not matter, because the binding
    constraint was never the rate of decay — it was attention. The fridge does
    not prevent waste. It relocates it: out of the shop, where you would have
    had to decide against buying, and into your own bin three weeks later, where
    time has made the decision for you and nobody has to feel they chose it.
  </p>
  <blockquote class="argument-verdict">
    You are not required to agree. You are required to argue, and the assessment
    rewards the student who presses the strongest objection — vaccine cold
    chains, reduced foodborne illness, year-round nutrition — over the one who
    agrees fluently.
  </blockquote>

  <h2>What you will do</h2>
  <p>
    This course cannot be passed from the library. Almost every week asks you to
    bring evidence from a real kitchen, usually your own, and the seminar runs
    on what you found rather than on what you read.
  </p>
  <dl class="activities">
    <div class="activities-row">
      <dt>Taste blind</dt>
      <dd>
        Fresh, three days refrigerated, and freeze-dried, without knowing which
        is which, and then argue about whether "freshness" is a physical state
        or a marketing achievement.
      </dd>
    </div>
    <div class="activities-row">
      <dt>Excavate a fridge</dt>
      <dd>
        Map a real interior as governed space, with its authority zones, its
        marginal shelves, and the unclaimed territory nobody will admit to
        owning.
      </dd>
    </div>
    <div class="activities-row">
      <dt>Run label forensics</dt>
      <dd>
        Bring ten confusing date labels, work out what the regulations actually
        require, and draft an honest replacement for each.
      </dd>
    </div>
    <div class="activities-row">
      <dt>Audit your own waste</dt>
      <dd>
        Record a week of everything you throw out, then cost it in money and in
        calories. This is the number that changes people.
      </dd>
    </div>
    <div class="activities-row">
      <dt>Design the dystopia</dt>
      <dd>
        Build the most exploitative smart fridge you can imagine, pitch it
        sincerely, then discover how much of it already exists.
      </dd>
    </div>
    <div class="activities-row">
      <dt>Make something that needs no plug</dt>
      <dd>
        A jar of quick ferment, or the operating rules for a community pantry.
      </dd>
    </div>
  </dl>
  <p>
    You leave with four things: an ethnography of a real household, a critical
    essay, a counter-fridge proposal you have defended in front of the room, and
    a jar of pickles.
  </p>

  <h2>The shape of the semester</h2>
  <p>
    Four modules of three weeks. Each one takes the same claim and turns it
    another degree.
  </p>
  <div class="modules">
    <div class="module">
      <p class="module-weeks">Weeks 1–3</p>
      <h3 class="module-title">
        <a href={withBase("/lectures/?module=1")}>Disenchantment</a>
      </h3>
      <p class="module-desc">
        The commodification of cold, from the ice trade to Freon, and the myth
        of modernity sold alongside it.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="module">
      <p class="module-weeks">Weeks 4–6</p>
      <h3 class="module-title">
        <a href={withBase("/lectures/?module=2")}>Micropolitics</a>
      </h3>
      <p class="module-desc">
        The interior as a jurisdiction, and the door as a stage.
      </p>
      <p class="module-due">
        Due: <a href={withBase("/assessments/fridge-ethnography/")}
          >Fridge Ethnography</a
        > (20%)
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="module">
      <p class="module-weeks">Weeks 7–9</p>
      <h3 class="module-title">
        <a href={withBase("/lectures/?module=3")}>The tyranny of time</a>
      </h3>
      <p class="module-desc">
        Date labels, systemic waste, and the stuffed fridge as emotional solace.
        Where the argument stops being interpretive and becomes measurable.
      </p>
      <p class="module-due">
        Due: <a href={withBase("/assessments/critical-essay/")}
          >Critical Essay</a
        > (30%)
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="module">
      <p class="module-weeks">Weeks 10–12</p>
      <h3 class="module-title">
        <a href={withBase("/lectures/?module=4")}
          >Resistance and reconstruction</a
        >
      </h3>
      <p class="module-desc">
        Surveillance capitalism in the kitchen, degrowth, fermentation, and what
        could be built instead.
      </p>
      <p class="module-due">
        Due: <a href={withBase("/assessments/counter-fridge/")}
          >Counter-Fridge</a
        > (40%) and{" "}
        <a href={withBase("/assessments/participation/")}>Participation</a> (10%)
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>Who it is for</h2>
  <p>
    A postgraduate seminar, open across disciplines. It suits anthropology,
    design, environmental studies, science and technology studies, food studies,
    sociology and media. There is no technical prerequisite and no assumed
    background in food science — the theory is taught as we go.
  </p>
  <p>
    What it does assume is a willingness to look at your own kitchen and report
    honestly what is in it, including the parts that are embarrassing. The
    fieldwork is not optional and it cannot be borrowed.
  </p>
  <p>Rule yourself out if you want:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>a food science or nutrition course — this is not one</li>
    <li>to learn to cook — you will make exactly one thing, badly</li>
    <li>
      to be told that refrigeration is simply good, or simply bad — the course
      argues neither and will make you defend whichever you arrive holding
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where to go next</h2>
  <CardGrid columns={2}>
    <Card
      title={sessionLabels.plural}
      href="/sessions/"
      image={sessionsHero}
      imageAlt="A fieldwork table seen at an angle: an open handwritten notebook, a kitchen scale, a mason jar mid-ferment, a tape measure, two magnifying glasses, a pencil, index cards and a screwdriver with a ruler, arranged across a teal tabletop on a warm cream ground."
    >
      <p>
        The twelve tutorials, and what to bring to each. This is where the
        course actually happens.
      </p>
    </Card>
    <Card
      title="Lectures"
      href="/lectures/"
      image={lecturesHero}
      imageAlt="A shelf of book spines in gold, teal and dark brown, seen straight on with a small hanging desk lamp lighting the row from above. Three blank index cards are tucked into the books, on a warm cream ground."
    >
      <p>
        The weekly argument and its readings, including decks for week 1 on the
        birth of artificial cold, week 7 on the tyranny of date labels, and the
        fifteen-minute week 12 wrap-up.
      </p>
    </Card>
    <Card
      title="Assessment"
      href="/assessments/"
      image={assessmentsHero}
      imageAlt="A fanned stack of marked paper, teal and gold folders showing between the sheets, a binder clip holding the top corner. The uppermost page carries handwritten notes with corrections in red ink, a grading stamp, and a percentage symbol, on a warm cream ground."
    >
      <p>
        An ethnography, an essay, a counter-fridge, and participation. What each
        is worth and when it is due.
      </p>
    </Card>
    <Card
      title="People"
      href="/people/"
      image={peopleHero}
      imageAlt="A wooden wall shelf holding a stoneware crock, a brass balance scale, glass apothecary bottles, a leather-bound notebook propped against a pressed-leaf print, a mortar and pestle, an ink bottle and a rolled scroll, with dried leaves scattered along the shelf, on a warm cream ground."
    >
      <p>
        Two convenors — one who reads objects, one who weighs what households
        throw away.
      </p>
    </Card>
    <Card
      title="Policies"
      href="/policies/"
      image={policiesHero}
      imageAlt="A wall of filing cabinets and box files seen at an angle, drawn in sepia ink. One drawer stands open, holding a thick stack of papers with a rubber stamp resting on top and a bundle of documents tied with string, on a warm cream ground."
    >
      <p>
        Fieldwork ethics, food safety, attendance, extensions and academic
        integrity — the rules this course runs on, and who to ask.
      </p>
    </Card>
  </CardGrid>
</ContentLayout>

<style>
  .course-tags {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    margin-block: 0 2rem;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .course-tags li {
    padding: 0.25rem 0.65rem;
    border-radius: 999px;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
    background: var(--at-bg-alt);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }

  /* Overrides the theme's default blockquote (top overbar, muted secondary
     text) with a left-border treatment in plain ink, sampled off the site's
     teal illustration accent rather than the gold brand primary — this is a
     verdict, not a citation, and gold is already spent on the emphasis above. */
  .argument-verdict {
    margin-inline: 0;
    padding-inline-start: var(--at-spacing-md);
    border-inline-start: 4px solid #3a575f;
    color: var(--at-text);
    font-weight: 400;
  }

  .argument-verdict::before {
    content: none;
  }

  .activities {
    margin-block: 0 var(--at-spacing-xl);
  }

  .activities-row {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(10rem, 16rem) 1fr;
    column-gap: var(--at-spacing-lg);
    padding-block: var(--at-spacing-md);
    border-block-start: 1px solid var(--at-divider);
  }

  .activities-row:last-child {
    border-block-end: 1px solid var(--at-divider);
  }

  .activities dt {
    font-weight: 700;
    color: var(--at-primary);
  }

  .activities dd {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--at-text);
  }

  @media (max-width: 40rem) {
    .activities-row {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      row-gap: var(--at-spacing-xs);
    }
  }

  .modules {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr);
    border: 1px solid var(--at-divider);
    margin-block: var(--at-spacing-lg) 0;
  }

  .module {
    position: relative;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    padding-block: var(--at-spacing-lg) var(--at-spacing-xs);
    padding-inline: var(--at-spacing-lg);
  }

  .module:nth-child(-n + 2) {
    border-block-end: 1px solid var(--at-divider);
  }

  .module:nth-child(odd) {
    border-inline-end: 1px solid var(--at-divider);
  }

  .module:nth-child(1) {
    --module-color: var(--at-primary);
  }
  .module:nth-child(2) {
    --module-color: oklch(from var(--at-primary) l c calc(h + 90));
  }
  .module:nth-child(3) {
    --module-color: oklch(from var(--at-primary) l c calc(h + 180));
  }
  .module:nth-child(4) {
    --module-color: oklch(from var(--at-primary) l c calc(h + 270));
  }

  .module:hover,
  .module:focus-within {
    background: light-dark(
      rgb(from var(--module-color) r g b / 8%),
      rgb(from var(--module-color) r g b / 16%)
    );
  }

  .module-weeks {
    margin-block: 0 var(--at-spacing-xs);
    font-size: var(--at-font-size-xs);
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--at-primary);
  }

  .module-title {
    margin-block: 0 var(--at-spacing-sm);
    color: var(--at-text);
  }

  .module-title a {
    color: inherit;
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  /* Stretched link: the title's own <a> gets an invisible overlay covering
     the whole card, so the entire module is clickable without nesting an
     <a> inside the .module-due link below (invalid HTML). The accessible
     name stays the title text, not a synthetic div click handler. */
  .module-title a::after {
    content: "";
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
  }

  .module-desc {
    margin: 0;
    min-height: 3lh;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
  }

  .module-due {
    margin-block-start: auto;
    padding-block-start: var(--at-spacing-sm);
    border-block-start: 1px solid var(--at-divider);
    font-size: var(--at-font-size-xs);
    font-weight: 700;
    color: var(--at-primary);
  }

  /* Lift above the title's stretched-link overlay so these stay clickable. */
  .module-due a {
    position: relative;
    z-index: 1;
  }

  @media (max-width: 40rem) {
    .modules {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }

    .module:nth-child(-n + 2) {
      border-block-end: none;
    }

    .module:not(:last-child) {
      border-block-end: 1px solid var(--at-divider);
    }

    .module:nth-child(odd) {
      border-inline-end: none;
    }
  }
</style>
